<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gresik</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gresik is a port town on the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, just north-west of Surabaya. From the fourteenth century it was a busy harbour for Chinese, Gujarati and Javanese traders and an early centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>: Maulana Malik Ibrahim was buried there in 1419, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunan-giri">Sunan Giri</a> founded his religious school on the hills nearby. It is now an industrial city.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gresik.jpg" alt="Gresik" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Everyone Sinks Starco · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Petrokimia_Gresik.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The town of Gresik, about 25 kilometres north-west of Surabaya, is the administrative centre of Gresik Regency in East Java Province. The regency takes in much of the northern and western suburbs of Surabaya and also Bawean Island, some 125 kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madura">Madura</a>, and almost a third of its area is coastal. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stamford-raffles">Thomas Stamford Raffles</a>, in The History of Java, derived the name from Giri Gisik, meaning mountain near the coast, after the hills of the town centre close to the shore.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Merchants from as far away as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> traded at the port of Gresik-Djaratan, and some of them helped spread <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in the area. The ancient Islamic tombs of Sheikh Maulana Malik Ibrahim and Fatimah bint Maimun mark the region as the first point of entry for Islam in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>. From the 14th century Gresik was one of the main ports and trading cities of the archipelago and a haven for ships sailing between Maluku, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> and mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, including India and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, a role it kept into the era of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">VOC</a>.</p><p>In 1487 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunan-giri">Sunan Giri</a>, also known as Sultan Ainul Yaqin, began to rule Gresik, and his descendants governed the area for the following two centuries. The Portuguese apothecary and traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tome-pires">Tomé Pires</a>, in his book Suma Oriental of 1515, called Gresik the jewel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> in trading ports. The area long formed part of Surabaya Regency until a government regulation of 1 November 1974 moved its administration to Gresik town under the new name of Gresik Regency. In the same year it became part of Gerbangkertosusila, the official metropolitan region of Surabaya.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The regency had 1,311,215 inhabitants at the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> and is divided into eighteen districts, two of which make up Bawean Island. Its largest works are Semen Gresik, a maker of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portland-cement">Portland cement</a>, and the fertiliser producer Petrokimia Gresik, while home industries make songkok caps and bags. The town is also known for its many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> shops, called warkop, from warung kopi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gresik Regency” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ports such as Tuban and <b>Gresik</b> were centres of domestic and international exchange and brought varied ethnic communities, including resident Chinese and Muslim merchants, into the imperial economy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…port cities of the north coast, which had grown rich on the spice and rice trade, Mataram drew its revenues mainly from land, and its conquest of the coast in the 1610s and 1620s brought devastation and depopulation to former trading centres such as Surabaya, Tuban and <b>Gresik</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mataram-sultanate">Mataram Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Later Ma Huan found large numbers of Chinese and Muslim merchants, from Arabia, India and above all Sumatra and the Malay Peninsula, settled in Tuban, <b>Gresik</b> and Hujung Galuh.</blockquote><a href="/societies/majapahit">Social Hierarchy of Majapahit</a></li><li><blockquote>At its height in the early 17th century it held influence over <b>Gresik</b>, Sedayu and parts of Borneo.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-surabaya">Duchy of Surabaya</a></li><li><blockquote>Sunan Giri, born Raden Paku and counted among the Wali Songo, founded a religious school on the hills of Giri near the port of <b>Gresik</b> in the late 15th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/giri-kedaton">Giri Kedaton</a></li></ul></section>
